examples.question_text:
$\frac{2}{3}$ of a length is $26$ cm.
(a) Find the whole length.
(b) A student calculates $26\times3$. Explain the missing step.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (6,0.8);
\draw (2,0)--(2,0.8);
\draw (4,0)--(4,0.8);
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=4pt}] (0,1)--(4,1);
\node[above] at (2,1.15) {$26$ cm};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=4pt}] (0,-0.2)--(6,-0.2);
\node[below] at (3,-0.35) {whole length $=$ ?};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
(a) One third: $26\div2=13$ cm
Three thirds: $13\times3=39$ cm
The whole length is $39$ cm.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (6,0.8);
\draw (2,0)--(2,0.8);
\draw (4,0)--(4,0.8);
\node at (1,0.4) {$13$ cm};
\node at (3,0.4) {$13$ cm};
\node at (5,0.4) {$13$ cm};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=4pt}] (0,1)--(4,1);
\node[above] at (2,1.15) {$26$ cm};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=4pt}] (0,-0.2)--(6,-0.2);
\node[below] at (3,-0.35) {whole length $=$ $39$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b) The known $26$ cm is two thirds, not one third. Divide by $2$ first to find one part, then multiply by $3$.
